{question}

{choices}

Instruction: Read the following excerpt from a proposed merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best captures the agreement's treatment of the "ability to consummate."\n\nQuestion: Is the “ability to consummate” concept exempt from the application of Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement Excerpt: "Material Adverse Effect" shall mean any event, incident, change, or series thereof that either (i) results in a material adverse effect on the overall business, financial condition, or operational performance of the Company and its affiliates, or (ii) directly prevents, materially impairs, or could reasonably be expected to materially delay the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. Notwithstanding the foregoing, for events described in clause (i) above, the occurrence of usual fluctuations in general economic indicators, movements in global financial markets, alterations in regulatory or political landscapes, or force majeure events including but not limited to natural disasters, pandemics, or acts of geopolitical instability shall not be deemed to constitute or contribute to a Material Adverse Effect. However, no such exclusions, limitations, or exceptions shall apply to events described in clause (ii) regarding the Company’s capacity to consummate the transaction, which shall be assessed in its entirety without relief from carveouts."
Choices:
